      <!-- ── SECTION 2 ── -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>Hair Mousse Polymers: VP/VA & Polyquaterniums</h2>
        <p>The core active ingredient in any hair mousse formulation is the styling resin. The most prevalent resin used in modern cosmetic aerosols is the VP copolymer hair aerosol system—specifically Polyvinylpyrrolidone/Vinyl Acetate (VP/VA) copolymer. This synthetic polymer forms a continuous, clear, and flexible film over the hair cuticles as the water and solvent evaporate. VP/VA is favoured because the ratio of VP to VA can be finely tuned; higher VP content yields excellent adhesion and washability but is highly hygroscopic (prone to absorbing moisture), whereas higher VA content provides superior humidity resistance. To enhance conditioning, formulators frequently blend these non-ionic resins with cationic Polyquaternium compounds (e.g., Polyquaternium-11 or Polyquaternium-4), which bind selectively to negatively charged, damaged hair shafts, providing anti-static properties and thermal protection.</p>
        
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Polymer Class</th>
                <th>Primary Function</th>
                <th>Typical Characteristics</th>
                <th>Common Examples</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Non-ionic (VP/VA)</td>
                <td>Film formation, primary hold</td>
