            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── SECTION 3 ── -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>Surfactant Selection for Emulsion Stability</h2>
        <p>In hair mousse aerosol technology, the surfactant system must perform a delicate balancing act. It must stabilize the liquid emulsion inside the can, preventing the non-polar <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2" target="_top">aerosol propellants</a> from separating out, while simultaneously generating a rich, creamy lather upon dispensing. Formulators typically rely on non-ionic surfactants, such as Polysorbate 20 or PEG-40 Hydrogenated Castor Oil, as well as mild amphoterics like Cocamidopropyl Betaine. These surfactants must be carefully selected to avoid reacting with the cationic conditioning polymers, which could cause precipitation. An unstable emulsion will lead to premature propellant expulsion or a "soupy" mousse that fails to expand into a structural foam.</p>

        <!-- GREEN CALLOUT -->
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Silicone-based surfactants (like PEG-12 Dimethicone) are increasingly used in premium hair mousse formulations. They act as exceptional foam stabilizers while simultaneously delivering high-slip conditioning benefits, drastically improving wet-combing without weighing down the hair.
        </div>
      </section>

